{{also|Cadence}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm}} {{term|cadence|lang=fr}} < {{etyl|it}} {{term|cadenza|lang=it}} < {{etyl|la}} {{term|cadentia|lang=la}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Balanced, rhythmic flow.
#* {{quote-news|year=1991|date=02/12|title=At the Saudi-Kuwaiti Border|work=ABC_Nightline|passage=Night has now passed in the Saudi desert and as we hear from Nightline correspondent Forrest Sawyer, the normal '''cadence''' of life at the front is about to change.}}
# The measure or beat of movement.
#* {{quote-journal|date=September|year=1993|volume=98, Iss. 5; page=102|title=Terror of the deep|author=Ken	Schultz|work=Field and Stream|passage=Getting into a good jigging rhythm means making short quick jerks in a regular '''cadence''' that might average about one jerk every 1.5 to 2 seconds.}}
# The general inflection or modulation of the voice.
#* {{quote-news|year=1991date=12/30|title=Raimey: A Breath of Fresh Ayah|author=David Holmstrom|work=Christian Science Monitor|passage=The '''cadence''' of Raimey's voice is pure Down-Easter Maine}}
# {{music}} A progression of at least two [[chord|chords]] which conclude a piece of [[music]], section or musical phrases within it. Sometimes referred to [[analogy|analogously]] as musical [[punctuation]].
# {{context|speech}} A fall in [[inflection]] of a speaker’s voice, such as at the end of a sentence.
# {{dance}} A dance move which ends a phrase.
#: ''The '''cadence''' in a galliard step refers to the final leap in a cinquepace sequence.''
# {{fencing}} The rhythm and sequence of a series of actions.
# {{context|running}} The number of steps per minute.
# {{cycling}} The number of revolutions per minute of the [[crank]]s or pedals of a [[bicycle]].
# {{military}} A chant that is sung by military personnel while running or marching; a [[jody call]].

